Mini Lab – Observe DNS in Action
Objective: See how your computer translates a website name into an IP address.
Windows
Open Command Prompt and type:
nslookup google.com
Linux/macOS
Open Terminal and type:
nslookup google.com
If nslookup isn't installed on Linux, you can also use:
dig google.com
Observe
Write down:
•	The website name
•	The IP address returned
•	The DNS server used for the lookup
Challenge
Repeat the command for:
•	youtube.com
•	github.com
•	openai.com
Compare the IP addresses. Notice that different websites have different IP addresses, and sometimes the same website can return different IPs due to load balancing.
________________________________________
Common Mistakes
•	Thinking the browser "knows" every website's address permanently.
•	Believing DNS stores website content (it only translates names to IP addresses).
•	Assuming a URL and an IP address are the same thing.
•	Thinking data is always sent as one large block instead of many packets.
________________________________________
Interview Preparation
Beginner
Question: What is DNS?
Why it's asked: DNS is one of the most fundamental networking concepts.
Ideal Answer: DNS (Domain Name System) translates human-readable domain names, such as google.com, into IP addresses that computers use to communicate.
Difficulty: 2/10
